#edb/ref 17592186366746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2943df26-2edc-5f92-bec7-1493042a2419" :question/math-academy-id "q-96595" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0d8df06-3a9e-5e41-94fc-efe4a82c5802" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Errors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049498 :topic/math-academy-id 3012 :topic/title "Type I and Type II Errors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28486acc-0e56-