ent :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83ac51a-0e3f-5990-8951-a42760df6d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399896} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99c72e-4b83-5737-a710-a4529e2905bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85b66c06-8b49-5d46-861f-2424f521305f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115479" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050480 :topic/math-academy-id 3219 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Directly Integrable Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4619988-524b-5cb0-826f-0876a1eba713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163595} {:answer/id #uuid "06b0f9bb-49e5-5d14-881c-b0c4eb0bcc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163596} {:answer/id #uuid "17a01152-f89c-540b-a3b6-945c4afbdf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163597} {:answer/id #uuid "1815a30a-345a-56eb-9b82-0d8040f43a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163598} {:answer/id #uuid "47590458-4f5b-5ec7-9265-75107a11e0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163599}] :answer-field/id #uuid "86103f0c-33cc-58bf-8251-67f27b6f8dc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163594}] :question/id #uuid "cffa78fe-45d2-5582-992b-e00f1609d3e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115548" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7ca20084e036941f9347a9dc0438fcd4f2d07cdd66bee75dc909de3ca07a0428.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x^{2} - 2x$\n2. $y^{′} = x^{2} - 4x + 4$\n3. $y^{′} = 2x - x^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722 :topic/math-academy-id 3220 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63fb830-9b70-5d30-9c38-66de7c477623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163601} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa96bfa-be09-5188-8b39-33b52ed9785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163602} {:answer/id #uuid "d0807f2c-c3a3-5255-bb74-a05557c94bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163603} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8a4af4-6213-59d6-92fa-5f03f1e21e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163604} {:answer/id #uuid "4244a138-3eb5-576f-ade8-ad33254d04ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163605}] :answer-field/id #uuid "60182852-8b4b-53e8-baca-d6c8425c477a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca235f3-bab6-576b-a9e8-170e50d79da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362902} {:answer/id #uuid "ac94547d-bd1e-5ec2-8f53-ba851ce7c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362903} {:answer/id #uuid "4611eb59-e37b-5e20-afcc-caaf2526e9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362904} {:answer/id #uuid "dfce9aa1-43d5-5f52-9e0b-3bf9f908fb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362905} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad754b-8713-53ba-93d9-fecbca23b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac94547d-bd1e-5ec2-8f53-ba851ce7c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362903} :answer-field/id #uuid "71cc3a1a-ecab-5f1d-9c09-2479a483b4b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50a7a912-e54a-503b-b768-f0c3f41e3a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-115856" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bba667daa6bfd40eeb5450cc86066184819b903ace2641bc58c6b5c83869a4b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4y+4$\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/72/72801ec62e5e23173097ef674169b26c85fab65cbf543af44b3582f0e14fd0c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=2$ only.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf6bc4f-ef3b-5a09-8060-fc268d13b58d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph and Properties of the Hyperbolic Secant" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049130 :topic/math-academy-id 3267 :topic/title "Graphs of the Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfa26e3-f62b-52a6-b9eb-19b01b1e298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163703} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b1100-67fd-5c48-a695-490178b96a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163704} {:answer/id #uuid "bd89675